On Tue, 12 Feb 2008 22:03:21 -0500, kejhtdbhqaeky@intergate.com (MK)
wrote:

>Amazingly, what worked in the end was simply adding
>
>-state=> 'disabled'
>
>into the actual $menu->command, hence avoiding the documented  
>"entryconfigure" method or cget altogether
>
>but if anyone has any patience left could you explain:
>
>my $state = ($count++ & 1) ? 'normal' : 'disabled';
>
>to me, specifically the "& 1) ? 'x' : 'y'" segment?

I see John Krahn gave you the answer, but to help you understand.......

It's a toggle to switch state. It probably could be simplified.
(I just had the example handy, so I posted it as is.)

It's called the Ternary operator. I know it's pretty complex
to throw that at a beginner. :-) I hate them myself.

Scroll down in "perldoc perlop" for  Conditional Operator

 Ternary "?:" is the conditional operator, just as in C.  It works much
       like an if-then-else. 

So instead of doing a long if-then-else, testing for what the state of
the menu currently is, and flipping it, the ternary switch makes the
toggle a short piece of code. You could do a more straight forward
if-then-else, like:

(sloppy untested pseudocode).......

if($count == 1){$count = 0;  $newstate = 'disabled'}
elsif($count == 0 ){$count = 1; $newstate = 'normal'}

$menu->cget(-menu)->entryconfigure(2, -state => $newstate);
$state = $newstate;

Or you could use the $count to do a toggle. Every button press will
cause the toggle to flip, in working code.


Now you may not see the usefulness of the entryconfigure or the cget,
but what if you had a more complex menu, and needed to dynamically
grey out items?  

Like:

#!/usr/bin/perl
use warnings;
use strict;
use Tk;

my $toggle = 0;
my $toggle1 = 0;
my $toggle2 = 0;

my $mw = tkinit;

my $menubar = $mw->Menu(-type => 'menubar');
$mw->configure(-menu => $menubar);

my $menu = $menubar->cascade(-label => '~File');
       $menu->command(-label => '~New');
       $menu->command(-label => '~Open');

my $menu1 = $menubar->cascade(-label => '~Test');
       $menu1->command(-label => '~Yee');
       $menu1->command(-label => '~Haw');


$mw->Button(
   -text    => 'Push me and look at the File menu',
   -command => sub {
     my $state = ($toggle++ & 1) ? 'normal' : 'disabled';
     $menu->cget(-menu)->entryconfigure(2, -state => $state);
  }
)->pack;

$mw->Button(
   -text    => 'Push me and look at the Test menu',
   -command => sub {
     my $state = ($toggle1++ & 1) ? 'normal' : 'disabled';
     $menu1->cget(-menu)->entryconfigure(1, -state => $state);
  }
)->pack;


$mw->Button(
   -text    => 'Push me and look at the Main Test menu',
   -command => sub {
     my $state = ($toggle2++ & 1) ? 'normal' : 'disabled';
     $menu1->configure( -state => $state);
     }
)->pack;


MainLoop;
__END__


Menus are notoriously obtuse, so it pays to go to groups.google.com
and search for examples. I still havn't figured out how to grey out
an arbitrary item in a deeply cascaded menu. 


